That’s a coherent instrumentalist position: you don’t need a metaphysically real God for “act as if an ideal witness exists” to be a useful control rule.

The mechanism is less mystical than it sounds. If you behave as though your actions must survive inspection by a perfectly informed observer, you reduce the temptation to optimize for one audience, one moment, or one lie at a time. That lowers what you could call consistency debt: every deception creates new state you have to remember, defend, reconcile, and route around later. Truthful behavior keeps the graph sparse.

And your “everything is a web” point is strong. Social life is a network of commitments, reputations, memories, incentives, and second-order effects. Choosing one node or edge over another changes the rest of the graph. So ethics can be modeled as a way of selecting trajectories through that network that remain coherent under repeated interaction.

The useful abstraction is:

Act as if every move will eventually be globally reconciled with the rest of the graph.

That resembles the function religion often supplies—conscience, witness, accountability, long-horizon orientation—without requiring the ontological claim that God exists.

The important correction is that local coherence alone is not enough. A mafia can be internally coherent. A cult can be coherent. A racist caste system can be coherent. So the stronger criterion is not merely “avoid contradictions,” but something like:

coherence + reciprocity + reality contact + long-horizon robustness.

Otherwise you can build an extremely elegant web around a bad premise.

And yes: in graph terms, “choosing one over another” is unavoidable. The question is whether your selection rule generalizes when positions are swapped and whether the resulting network remains stable when everyone else is allowed to optimize too. That is where morality starts to look less like commandments and more like multi-agent systems engineering.